<p><em>Theodore Roosevelt Roughrider</em> portrays North Dakota's most famous adopted son as colonel of the regiment he commanded during the Spanish-American War. Sculptor {% person "alexander-phimister-proctor" %}Alexander Phimister Proctor{% endperson %} was born in Canada and raised in Colorado. A contemporary of {% person "frederic-remington" %}Frederic Remington{% endperson %} and {% person "charles-m-russell" %}Charles Russell{% endperson %}, Proctor dedicated his life to monumental sculpture. His other equestrian works include statues of General {% person "william-t-sherman" %}William T. Sherman{% endperson %} in New York's {% place "central-park-new-york" %}Central Park{% endplace %} and General {% person "robert-e-lee" %}Robert E. Lee{% endperson %} in Dallas, as well as Denver's <em>Bronco Buster</em>.</p>

<p>Proctor's original Roosevelt statue was erected in Portland, Oregon, and became the subject of the {% place "metropolitan-museum-of-art" %}Metropolitan Museum of Art{% endplace %}'s first educational film about a sculptor's work. Proctor met {% person "theodore-roosevelt" %}Theodore Roosevelt{% endperson %} at the 1893 World's Fair, and Roosevelt later commissioned him to create several sculptures for the {% place "white-house" %}White House{% endplace %}.</p>

<p>According to the Inventory of American Sculpture, Mandan's sculpture is a smaller version of the one commissioned by {% person "henry-waldo-coe" %}Dr. Henry Waldo Coe{% endperson %} for Portland. Proctor remarked about his intention: "I wanted to give the world... the impression of Roosevelt as I knew him --- as, indeed, I always think of him. I most admired his fearlessness, his courage, and the energy always waiting to spring into action. I never thought of him as a man of hasty, ill-considered action. All his exuberance, all his restlessness, was only the surface that covered a quiet dignity and reserve. The popular idea of him is that he never was in repose. I wanted to show him as he appeared to me, with all his magnificent energy held in check."</p>

<p>Coe met Roosevelt while living in Dakota Territory, and they formed a lasting friendship. Coe represented Mandan residents in the last territorial legislature in 1885 and served as the city's eighth mayor in 1888. He and his wife moved to Portland in 1891. After commissioning the original statue for Portland, he gave the smaller version to Mandan.</p>

<p>The Roosevelt family approved the design. {% person "edith-roosevelt" %}Edith Roosevelt{% endperson %} and {% person "kermit-roosevelt" %}Kermit Roosevelt{% endperson %} found the uniform Roosevelt wore at {% place "san-juan-hill-cuba" %}San Juan Hill{% endplace %} in family trunks and provided it to Proctor as a reference in casting the statue. Roosevelt's widow, all five surviving children, and their spouses attended the dedication.</p>

